One-hot encoding was employed in this study to represent input amino acid residues. Specifically, each amino acid residue is encoded as a binary vector of fixed length, where only one position corresponding to the specific residue is set to 1, while all other positions are set to 0. Consequently, an entire amino acid sequence can be encoded as a two-dimensional matrix of size [\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
