The ESM-2 model utilizes self-supervised learning on large-scale protein sequence databases, primarily the UniRef [42] database and MGnify [43]. Protein sequences are processed through multiple layers of Transformer encoders, which employ self-attention mechanisms and feed-forward neural networks [44]. The model can generate latent feature representations for each amino acid residue [45], capturing both syntactic and semantic information within the sequence, as well as interactions between residues and three-dimensional structural information [26]. In this study, we employed ESM-2 3B model to embed the peptide sequences, representing each sequence as a two-dimensional matrix of size [\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
